// ATTACK (red team round 4, concurrency lens): does the closure-lock
// self-reentrancy DETECTOR (kryos_closure_lock_acquire, thread-local
// HashMap<usize,()> keyed by lock address, LEDGER "closure-lock-self-
// reentrancy-hang") generalize to INDIRECT reentry -- A calls B, B calls
// back into the SAME A instance -- or was it only exercised/verified for
// the DIRECT self-call shape (f calling f through its own stored handle)?
//
// If detection is keyed correctly by lock address (A's env pointer), the
// second entry into A on the same thread (via B) must be refused exactly
// like direct self-recursion: a clean `kryos panic: reentrant call into a
// mutating shared closure: ...`, exit 98. If detection is somehow scoped
// to the CALL SITE or the immediately-enclosing thunk rather than the lock
// address globally, this could instead hang forever (A's real
// kryos_mutex_lock CAS spins against a lock the same thread already holds,
// with no owner-thread tracking to break the spin) -- the exact permanent
// hang item 11(a) was supposed to have eliminated for every same-thread
// path back into a closure's own lock, not just the direct one.
@capabilities(all)
fn main() {
    let mut handlers: map<i64, fn(i64) -> i64> = {}
    let mut hits: i64 = 0
    let a = |n: i64| {
        hits = hits + 1
        if n > 0 {
            let b = handlers[1]
            b(n - 1)
        } else {
            hits
        }
    }
    let b = |n: i64| {
        if n > 0 {
            let aa = handlers[0]
            aa(n - 1)
        } else {
            -1
        }
    }
    handlers[0] = a
    handlers[1] = b
    println("about to call a(3) -- indirect A->B->A reentry, if this hangs the detector does not generalize")
    let result = a(3)
    println("UNREACHABLE if detection generalizes: result=" + to_string(result))
}
